WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.829
One thread, many tasks. How Node serves a crowd while doing one thing at a time.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.980
Your JavaScript runs on one thread. Picture one cook at one counter.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:15.857
The counter is the call stack: the function running right now.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:20.102
A call puts a frame on the counter. A return takes it off.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.191
But the cook never stands waiting for the oven.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.981
Timers and file or network work belong to libuv and the operating system.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:34.306
They never interrupt the cook. When they finish, they put a ticket on a rail.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.906
There are two rails. Promise callbacks go on the microtask rail.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.264
Timer and I/O callbacks go on the task rail.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.761
The event loop is the cook, glancing at the rails.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.526
But only when the counter is empty.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.352
First, every microtask. Then one task. Then the rails again.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.318
Four lines. script start prints straight away.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:06.633
setTimeout hands its callback to the timer. The ticket reaches the task rail.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:11.107
Then .then() on a finished promise puts a ticket on the microtask rail.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.830
script end is printed. The counter is empty.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:21.331
Now the cook looks. Microtasks first: promise. Then the task: timeout.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:28.276
The timer said 0 milliseconds, yet it ran last. A delay is a minimum, not an appointment.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.479
Now a harder one. Predict the order before you look.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.304
1 and 6 are plain calls. They print first.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.587
.then() is a microtask, so 4 comes next. It schedules another timer.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:49.314
Then the first timer's task prints 2, and queues a microtask. That prints 3 before the next task.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:56.386
Last, the second timer: 5. 1, 6, 4, 2, 3, 5.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.754
Now give the cook a long job. A loop that runs for 200 milliseconds.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.123
The timer finishes at once. Its ticket waits on the rail.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:13.386
Nothing interrupts the cook, so nothing else runs. In a server, every request waits.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:18.899
Long synchronous work blocks. Waiting for a database or a fetch does not.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.491
So how do you wait without blocking? With await.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.176
In checkHealth, await fetch() sends the request, and the function pauses.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.534
The cook is free. Other requests run.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.321
When the response arrives, the rest of the function is a microtask on the rail.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:40.340
await means: pause this function, free the cook.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.880
Practise it with the event-loop visualiser in Server-side JavaScript.
